.TH EFIBOOTMGR 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
efibootmgr \- show and change the EEPROM's boot entries and boot order
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B efibootmgr
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.RB [ \-c
.B \-d
.I disk
.RB [ \-l
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-L
.IR label ]]
.RB [ \-b
.I XXXX
.RB [ \-B ]]
.RB [ \-o
.I X,Y
.RB | " \-O" ]
.RB [ \-n
.I X
.RB | " \-N" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The EEPROM keeps boot variables in its NVRAM, as a UEFI board does:
boot entries
.RB ( Boot0000
to
.BR Boot0007 ),
each a disk by its id and a file on it;
.BR BootOrder ,
the order the entries are tried in;
.BR BootNext ,
an entry tried once, at the next boot only; and
.BR BootCurrent ,
the entry that booted the computer this time (none when it booted by the
fallback), kept until it's off.
At power-on the EEPROM's bootloader tries
.B BootNext
(and clears it), then each entry of
.B BootOrder
whose disk is on the bus, then every disk in bus order holding
.BR /init.lua .
.PP
With no options,
.B efibootmgr
shows the variables. After a change it shows them as they are now, unless
.BR \-q .
Entry numbers are hexadecimal, as in
.BR Boot0001 .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-create
Makes a new entry, numbered
.BR \-b 's
or the first free one, and puts it first in
.BR BootOrder .
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-disk " \fIdisk\fR"
The new entry's disk: its name in
.B /dev
.RB ( storage1 ,
.BR /dev/storage1 ),
or its id
.RB ( id:\fIuuid\fR ),
which may name a disk that isn't on the bus yet.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-loader " \fIpath\fR"
The file the new entry boots;
.B /init.lua
when not given.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-label " \fIlabel\fR"
The new entry's label.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bootnum " \fIXXXX\fR"
The entry
.B \-c
makes or
.B \-B
deletes.
.TP
.BR \-B ", " \-\-delete\-bootnum
Deletes entry
.BR \-b 's,
from
.B BootOrder
and
.B BootNext
too.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-bootorder " \fIX,Y\fR"
Sets
.BR BootOrder .
.TP
.BR \-O ", " \-\-delete\-bootorder
Clears
.BR BootOrder .
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-bootnext " \fIX\fR"
Boots entry
.I X
next time only.
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-delete\-bootnext
Clears
.BR BootNext .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Shows each entry's disk and file, and
.B (not present)
for a disk that isn't on the bus.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Shows nothing after a change.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when done; 1 when the EEPROM refuses a change (why is on standard error:
an id that isn't one, a path that isn't absolute, an entry that doesn't
exist), or there's no EEPROM; 2 for bad usage.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/eeprom
The EEPROM, whose ioctls change the variables.
.TP
.B /sys/firmware/efi/efivars
The variables, a file each, to read.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
efibootmgr -v
efibootmgr -c -d storage1 -L "tiny OS"
efibootmgr -n 1
efibootmgr -o 1,0
efibootmgr -b 1 -B
.fi
.SH NOTES
Linux's efibootmgr writes UEFI's variables, which name a partition by its
GUID and a file on it; mcos's name a disk by its id. The EEPROM checks
every change, so a variable is always one its bootloader can read.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR label (1),
.BR lsdev (1)
